{"edits": [{"finding_ids": ["app/api_errors.py:10:validator"], "search": "MESSAGE_CODES = {
    "field required": "MISSING",
    "value is not a valid integer": "NOT_AN_INTEGER",
    "ensure this value has at least": "TOO_SHORT",
}", "replace": "MESSAGE_CODES = {
    "missing": "MISSING",
    "int_parsing": "NOT_AN_INTEGER",
    "string_too_short": "TOO_SHORT",
}", "explanation": "The failing test shows every error mapped to INVALID. The v1 message strings ("field required", "value is not a valid integer", "ensure this value has at least") no longer exist in Pydantic v2, whose messages read e.g. "Field required" and "Input should be a valid integer...". Pydantic v2 errors expose a stable machine-readable `type` field ("missing", "int_parsing", "string_too_short"), so the keys are switched to those types, which is the documented way to branch on error kinds in v2 rather than matching message text. Public codes are unchanged."}, {"finding_ids": ["app/api_errors.py:10:validator"], "search": "            code = next((c for m, c in MESSAGE_CODES.items() if err["msg"].startswith(m)), "INVALID")", "replace": "            code = MESSAGE_CODES.get(err["type"], "INVALID")", "explanation": "Lookup now matches the exact v2 error `type` instead of a prefix of the human-readable `msg`, which changed between v1 and v2. Unknown types still fall back to "INVALID", preserving the existing fallback behavior."}], "notes": "The failing test was not caused by the @field_validator migration, which is already correct (v2 decorator with @classmethod). The real failure is that error_codes() matched v1 message text ("field required", etc.) against v2 messages, so every error fell through to "INVALID". The fix maps on the stable v2 error `type` values instead. The expected outputs (username -> MISSING, age -> NOT_AN_INTEGER) follow from the types "missing" and "int_parsing". The `username` min_length error maps to "string_too_short" -> TOO_SHORT, as before. The previously applied field_validator migration and the imports are left untouched, since `Field`, `ValidationError`, `BaseModel`, and `field_validator` are all still used. I did not run the test; the mapping is based on the Pydantic v2 error type names."}
